{% extends "_layout.jinja" %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "_icons.jinja" as icons %}
{% block title %}Speedfit (preview, not installable) · Cuprum{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Speedfit is a proposed companion to Cuprum: an offline compiler that turns command descriptions into typed Python functions returning Cuprum commands. Design draft only; nothing to install.{% endblock %}
{% block main_class %} class="cu-speedfit"{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
{% set sections = [
  {'id': 'boundary', 'label': 'The boundary', 'title': 'Describe, generate, run',
   'lede': 'Three owners, and generation is not execution. Speedfit produces argv; Cuprum decides whether and how it runs.'},
  {'id': 'illustrative', 'label': 'Illustrative API', 'title': 'What a generated adapter looks like',
   'lede': 'A handwritten stand-in from the design documents, not generated output and not a package you can install.'},
  {'id': 'grammar', 'label': 'Grammar', 'title': 'Not every command is flat',
   'lede': 'Commands fall into grammar classes, and each class changes what a trustworthy serializer needs.'},
  {'id': 'exclusions', 'label': 'Exclusions', 'title': 'What Speedfit will not do'},
  {'id': 'open', 'label': 'Open questions', 'title': 'Decisions still to make'},
] %}
{% set crate_art = {
  'name': 'fittings-crate', 'widths': [768, 1536], 'width': 1536, 'height': 1024,
  'alt': '', 'plate': 'Plate 08',
  'caption': 'Copper inspects a fitting from a crate sorted by kind. Speedfit is about naming the fittings; the tags are blank because the corpus is not written yet.',
  'eager': true,
} %}
{% set boundary = {
  'id': 'fig-speedfit', 'num': 'Fig. 01', 'horizontal': true, 'status': 'proposed',
  'title': 'Who describes a command, who generates it, and who runs it?',
  'steps': [
    {'kind': 'builder', 'kind_label': '1 · Source description', 'name': 'completion specs + overlays', 'detail': 'Versioned command metadata, imported statically, corrected by curated overlays for a named target profile.', 'edge': 'evidence-bearing IR'},
    {'kind': 'builder', 'kind_label': '2 · Generated adapter', 'name': 'def echo(*text, n=False) -> SafeCmd', 'detail': 'An ordinary typed function that serializes values into an exact argv. It runs nothing.', 'edge': 'SafeCmd'},
    {'kind': 'process', 'kind_label': '3 · Cuprum runtime', 'name': 'policy, run, pipelines, results', 'detail': 'Allowlists, scopes, execution, observation, cancellation, and results, exactly as for any SafeCmd.'},
  ],
  'branches': [
    {'when': 'Build time', 'then': 'The compiler never executes imported completion code under its default policy, and a command with unresolved facts is not published.'},
    {'when': 'Run time', 'then': 'The generated package imports no compiler, Node.js, or Fig code, and never inspects PATH.'},
  ],
  'takeaway': 'Speedfit names the fittings. Cuprum runs the pipes.',
  'source': 'Source: Speedfit technical design, draft v0.1 (18 July 2026), Figure 1',
} %}
      {% call ui.page_head('Annex S · Preview · Not yet installable', 'Speedfit',
        'Speedfit names the fittings; Cuprum runs the pipes. It is a proposed offline compiler that turns command descriptions into typed Python functions, each returning an exact Cuprum SafeCmd.',
        statuses=['proposed', 'illustrative'],
        meta='Design draft v0.1, last revised 18 July 2026 · no code, package, or public repository yet',
        art_image=crate_art, stamp='Not yet installable') %}
        {{ ui.button('See the boundary', '#boundary', icon='arrow-down') }}
        {{ ui.button('Cuprum on GitHub', repository_url, variant='outline', icon='external') }}
      {% endcall %}
      {{ ui.routemap(sections, 'Annex S') }}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'boundary') %}
      {{ ui.flow(boundary) }}
      <div class="cu-columns mt-12">
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Why a compiler</h3>
          <p>
            Cuprum’s <code>tar</code> builder validates its values and
            constructs a canonical tuple of tokens by hand. That works, but
            every supported command needs new handwritten API, validation,
            serialization, documentation, and tests. Meanwhile completion
            projects such as Fig, Carapace, and OpenCLI already describe the
            structure of many popular command lines.
          </p>
          <p>
            Speedfit proposes to close that gap: import what those sources can
            prove, correct it with curated overlays, and generate typed
            functions a checker can hold to account.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Why not trust completion data as is</h3>
          <p>
            Completion asks what token might sensibly come next. Invocation
            asks which typed values form a valid command, and exactly how they
            become argv. A completion spec may suggest numbers without proving
            the program accepts an integer, or which separator it wants, or
            which version introduced the option.
          </p>
          <p>
            So every field in Speedfit’s intermediate representation carries
            its evidence — authoritative, curated, imported, inferred, or
            unresolved — and unresolved fields block publication.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'illustrative', class='cu-section--tint') %}
      {% call ui.callout('preview', 'Illustrative API') %}
      <p>
        The adapter below is handwritten to show the shape the compiler would
        generate. It is ordinary Python built on Cuprum, so the argv it prints
        is real — it was run against main@{{ cuprum_ref }} on
        {{ cuprum_verified_on }} — but there is no <code>speedfit</code>
        package, and the API may change entirely.
      </p>
      {% endcall %}
      <div class="cu-pair mt-8">
        {% call ui.code_panel('generated_api_example.py (excerpt)', 'Illustrative') %}
        {% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
def echo(
    *text: TextOperand,
    n: bool = False,
    enable_escapes: bool = False,
    disable_escapes: bool = False,
) -> SafeCmd:
    """Write operands with the selected GNU ``echo`` profile."""

    if enable_escapes and disable_escapes:
        raise ValueError("enable_escapes and disable_escapes conflict")

    operands = tuple(_stringify(value, role="echo operand") for value in text)
    if operands and _looks_like_echo_option(operands[0]):
        msg = (
            f"ambiguous first echo operand {operands[0]!r}; the selected profile "
            "has no verified option terminator for this position"
        )
        raise ValueError(msg)

    argv: list[str] = []
    if n:
        argv.append("-n")
    if enable_escapes:
        argv.append("-e")
    if disable_escapes:
        argv.append("-E")
    argv.extend(operands)
    return _BUILD_ECHO(*argv)
        {% endhighlight %}
        {% endcall %}
        <div class="cu-stack">
          {% call ui.code_panel('use_adapters.py', 'Illustrative', foot=['Run ' ~ cuprum_verified_on ~ ' against main@' ~ cuprum_ref], flat=true) %}
          {% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import functools

from generated_api_example import BlockSize, Conversion, dd, echo

backup = dd(
    input_path="disk.img",
    output_path="backup.img",
    block_size=BlockSize("4M"),
    conversions=(Conversion.NOERROR, Conversion.SYNC),
)
print(backup.argv_with_program)

quiet = functools.partial(echo, n=True)
print(quiet("hello", "pipes").argv_with_program)

try:
    echo("-n")
except ValueError as exc:
    print(f"ValueError: {exc}")
          {% endhighlight %}
          {% endcall %}
          {% call ui.output('Exact argv', 'stdout') %}
('dd', 'if=disk.img', 'of=backup.img', 'bs=4M', 'conv=noerror,sync')
('echo', '-n', 'hello', 'pipes')
ValueError: ambiguous first echo operand '-n'; the selected profile has no verified option terminator for this position
          {% endcall %}
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cu-pair mt-10">
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <h3>Reading it</h3>
          <p>
            <code>dd</code> takes assignment operands, so its parameters become
            <code>if=</code>, <code>of=</code>, <code>bs=</code>, and
            <code>conv=</code> tokens rather than flags. <code>echo</code> is
            wrapped with <code>functools.partial</code> and keeps a checkable
            signature. And an operand the GNU profile could read as an option
            is refused before anything runs, because this profile has no
            verified way to terminate options.
          </p>
        </div>
        {{ ui.facts([
          ('Source lock', 'Each source repository, immutable revision, selected files, checksums, and adapter version'),
          ('Licence', 'Licence evidence for every imported source, carried into the generated package'),
          ('Overlays', 'The curated overlay set applied on top of imported facts'),
          ('Evidence', 'Per field: authoritative, curated, imported, inferred, or unresolved'),
          ('Reproducible', 'Two builds from the same lock produce byte-identical source and provenance'),
        ], 'Provenance each generated package records') }}
      </div>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'grammar') %}
      {% call ui.matrix('grammar-caption', 'Command grammar classes · from the Speedfit terms of reference',
        ['Grammar class', 'Examples', 'Consequence for Speedfit']) %}
      <tr role="row">
        <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Flat option algebra</th>
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Examples') %}<code>echo</code>, many core utilities{% endcall %}
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Consequence for Speedfit') %}A generated function can usually model the full command.{% endcall %}
      </tr>
      <tr role="row">
        <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Assignment operands</th>
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Examples') %}<code>dd</code>{% endcall %}
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Consequence for Speedfit') %}Named values serialize as tokens such as <code>bs=4M</code>, not POSIX flags.{% endcall %}
      </tr>
      <tr role="row">
        <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Command family</th>
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Examples') %}<code>git</code>, <code>docker</code>{% endcall %}
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Consequence for Speedfit') %}Runnable leaves map to functions in a module.{% endcall %}
      </tr>
      <tr role="row">
        <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Mode-sensitive command</th>
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Examples') %}<code>tar</code>, <code>openssl</code>{% endcall %}
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Consequence for Speedfit') %}Incompatible modes become separate functions.{% endcall %}
      </tr>
      <tr role="row">
        <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Embedded language</th>
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Examples') %}<code>find</code>, <code>sed</code>{% endcall %}
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Consequence for Speedfit') %}A command-specific syntax tree is required.{% endcall %}
      </tr>
      <tr role="row">
        <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Delegated execution</th>
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Examples') %}<code>find -exec</code>, <code>sh -c</code>{% endcall %}
        {% call ui.matrix_cell('Consequence for Speedfit') %}Authorization must cover programs nested inside arguments. Excluded for now.{% endcall %}
      </tr>
      {% endcall %}
      <p class="cu-prose mt-6">
        Dialect is part of the contract. GNU, BSD, BusyBox, and vendor variants
        are separate target profiles, never folded into a fictional universal
        command, and Speedfit does not guess one from the machine it runs on.
      </p>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'exclusions', class='cu-section--tint') %}
      <div class="cu-speedfit__exclusions">
        {% call ui.callout('caveat', 'Delegated execution is not included') %}
        <p>
          <code>find -exec</code>, <code>sh -c</code>, and command-valued
          options make one program run another. Cuprum authorizes a
          <code>SafeCmd</code> by its direct program, so allowing
          <code>find</code> would quietly allow whatever it executes. These
          features stay out of the publishable surface until Cuprum can
          authorize the whole set of programs a command may start.
        </p>
        {% endcall %}
        {% call ui.callout('caveat', 'Secrets stay out of argv') %}
        <p>
          A type annotation does not make a process argument confidential:
          argv is visible to other processes and reaches logs and traces.
          Password, token, and key parameters are excluded from the default
          surface until a sensitivity and redaction policy exists.
        </p>
        {% endcall %}
        {% call ui.callout('note', 'Not a shell, not a runtime') %}
        <p>
          No shell strings, globbing, redirection, or control flow. No runtime
          discovery of commands from PATH. No execution, installation, or
          process supervision: Speedfit generates argv, and Cuprum owns
          everything that happens after.
        </p>
        {% endcall %}
      </div>
      {% endcall %}

      {% call ui.section(sections, 'open') %}
      <ol class="cu-invariants cu-speedfit__questions">
        <li><strong>Which target profile ships first?</strong> GNU/Linux, macOS/BSD, BusyBox, or a narrower pinned toolchain decides the semantics, fixtures, and package naming.</li>
        <li><strong>Which commands form the first corpus?</strong> The proposal is <code>echo</code>, <code>dd</code>, selected <code>git</code> leaves, <code>tar</code> create and extract, and a non-delegating <code>find</code> subset.</li>
        <li><strong>May derived Fig material be redistributed?</strong> The examined revision’s licence files disagree, so this needs a written interpretation first.</li>
        <li><strong>How should Cuprum authorize delegated execution?</strong> A joint Speedfit and Cuprum decision; it blocks <code>-exec</code>-style features, not a safe first subset.</li>
        <li><strong>One package, per-profile extras, or generate-it-yourself?</strong> The choice sets release cadence, licence obligations, and compatibility promises.</li>
        <li><strong>Is the name available?</strong> <code>speedfit</code> must be checked across PyPI, source hosts, and trademarks before anything is published.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="cu-speedfit__follow mt-12">
        <div class="cu-prose">
          <p>
            There is no Speedfit repository to star and no package to install.
            Speedfit targets Cuprum’s runtime, so Cuprum’s repository is the
            place to follow the seam it depends on and to ask about it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
          {{ ui.button('Cuprum issues', repository_url ~ '/issues', icon='external') }}
          {{ ui.button('Cuprum builders today', repository_url ~ '/blob/main/docs/users-guide.md#core-builders-for-common-tools', variant='outline', icon='external') }}
        </div>
      </div>
      {% endcall %}
{% endblock %}
